APIs, you're actually

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.730
able to improve your
overall business model

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:02.440
and accelerate your business and
transform your IT department.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.090
We have one really
awesome feature

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.220
known as live migration,
and that's the ability

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.230
to basically move
virtual machines

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.347
from one underlying host
to another underlying host.

00:06:11.347 --> 00:06:13.680
VINCENT WIENCZORKOWSKI: So,
Nick, from a live migration,

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.120
it seems like an
interesting feature.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:17.760
But how, operationally,
is that really

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.720
going to change the
way I'm doing business?

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.400
How is that going to impact
our customers to improve

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.532
upon what they're doing?

00:06:24.532 --> 00:06:26.490
NICK PAN: Well, that's
a really great question.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:27.660
In terms of the
start of the year,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:29.280
I'm sure many of
you in the audience

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.500
have heard of the CPU
vulnerabilities that

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:32.920
occurred in January--

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:34.320
Meltdown and Spectre.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:36.300
These were CPU
vulnerabilities where

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.950
there was a very high severity
and a very business critical

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.530
need to patch your
CPUs and make sure

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:47.700
that we reduce the risk of the
security as much as possible.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.350
With live migration,
Google Cloud

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:53.430
was actually able to patch
all of our underlying fleet

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.610
without our customers actually
noticing any sort of downtime

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.080
or patching done
to those instances.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.770
So as you start using
Google Cloud's technology,

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.210
you inherit all
of these benefits

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:07.660
and employ a very
secure security posture.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.830
Also, we have a very customer
friendly pricing model.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.410
So we have features such as
sustained use discounts, which

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.690
are automatic discounts
on our virtual machines

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:16.950
as you use them.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.720
And generally, we
see customers who

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.030
are using Google Cloud
Platform to save around

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:24.470
30% on virtual machines
a month, just based off

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.180
of using those automatic
sustained use discounts.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.000
We also have the idea of
custom machine types, which

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.730
are the ability to fine
tune your configuration

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.680
of your virtual machines
in terms of number of CPUs

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.560
as well as gigabytes
of RAM in order

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.860
to really cater your
machine shapes to meet

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.670
the needs of your applications.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:44.045
VINCENT WIENCZORKOWSKI:
So, Nick,

00:07:44.045 --> 00:07:47.315
that's interesting just from a
more flexible business model.

00:07:47.315 --> 00:07:50.880
But what does that actually
mean for a retail customer

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.870
or somebody in media and
how sustained use discounts

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:56.940
and custom machine types
can impact their business

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.855
operations and affect
their bottom line?

00:07:59.855 --> 00:08:01.480
NICK PAN: So in terms
of in industries,

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:05.050
when you think about things like
using cloud for burst capacity,

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:06.990
often times you're not
actually committing

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:09.030
to a particular instance
type or a reservation

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:10.649
in terms of pulls of compute.

00:08:10.649 --> 00:08:12.690
With that, you're actually
able to have sustained

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.900
use discounts automatically
applied to that burst capacity,

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.970
and have real time
discounts based off

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:21.739
of those fluctuations of
usage for your applications.

00:08:21.739 --> 00:08:24.030
Additionally, for our customers
who are looking to lift

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:25.890
and improve-- not
lift and shift--

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:28.470
we actually see them not
understanding, really,

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:29.970
how the cloud works
yet, but they're

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:33.809
able to benefit from
sustained usage discounts

00:08:33.809 --> 00:08:35.400
because they're not
having to commit

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.590
to anything at that
point, and they're just

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:41.380
inheriting that sustained
use discount as they ramp up.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.950
So overall, increasing
agility, having a more customer

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:45.990
friendly business
model, and ensuring

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.117
that we're giving all the
controls to the customer

00:08:48.117 --> 00:08:49.950
to make sure you're
making the right choices

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:52.210
in terms of price, as
well as performance.

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:54.626
VINCENT WIENCZORKOWSKI: So
basically, true cloud economics

00:08:54.626 --> 00:08:56.670
and no guesswork in
how to get discounting.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:57.570
NICK PAN: Exactly.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:00.330
And we believe that the bread
and butter of Google Cloud that

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:03.810
makes this so powerful
is our underlying global

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:04.452
infrastructure.

00:09:04.452 --> 00:09:05.910
If you look at this
slide, we could

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:07.950
see that this is our
global footprint.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.020
As I said before, 15 regions.

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:13.050
We maintain one of the largest
backbones on the internet

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:13.980
currently.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:15.660
Statistics show
from third parties

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:19.200
that we probably have around 30%
of the world's internet traffic

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.330
pass through our backbone
on any given day.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:24.330
And what this empowers
you as a developer or you

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:27.540
as a business leader is being
able to harness this network

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:31.080
to host your applications
either closer to your users

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.960
or to reduce latency,
as well as the overall

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:37.030
hops that it takes
to reach your users,

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.559
as well as have system
to system traffic.

00:09:39.559 --> 00:09:41.850
So very powerful for increasing
that network throughput

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:43.320
and that network bandwidth.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.660
And we really believe
Google Cloud is a place

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:48.390
to build multi-regional,
globally distributed

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.520
systems on the cloud.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.890
And this really shows
you where the rubber

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.420
meets the road,
where it actually

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:58.110
shows you instances doing
network diagnostic tests

00:09:58.110 --> 00:09:59.710
between two regions.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:02.970
So I went ahead and spun up
a virtual machine in Oregon,

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:04.980
as well as a virtual
machine in Japan,

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.080
and did a ping as
well as a traceroute

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:09.970
across, bi-directionally
from those instances.

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:13.110
If you could see on the bottom,
there is actually only one hop

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:16.140
that it traverses when
it goes between our VMs.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:19.110
And the reasons for that is
because we operate our network

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:21.600
backbone, and we're able
to hold onto that traffic

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:25.080
for a majority of the route and
ensure that you have a secure,

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.210
consistent, as well
as reliable route

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:29.252
as you pass through the
internet on our backbone.

00:10:29.252 --> 00:10:31.293
VINCENT WIENCZORKOWSKI:
So, Nick, what I'm saying

00:10:31.293 --> 00:10:33.520
is, whether or not you
are building an isolated

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:35.370
application in
one of our regions

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:37.480
or building a
distributed application

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:39.150
in multiple regions,
you're going

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:41.310
to benefit from
staying on our backbone

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:43.246
from a security and a
consistent performance

00:10:43.246 --> 00:10:44.370
from a networks standpoint.

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:44.900
Is that right?

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:45.650
NICK PAN: Exactly.

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:48.330
So we don't have to actually
push traffic to a transit ISP

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:49.680
or transit provider.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:51.750
We operate our backbone
and are able to ensure

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.017
that performance.

00:10:53.017 --> 00:10:54.600
And along with all
of this, we believe

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:58.390
that we very much excel in
security across the board

00:10:58.390 --> 00:10:59.590
at Google Cloud.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:03.740
So we employ a defense in depth
at scale by default posture.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.490
So from the hardware
level in terms

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:09.660
of the actual infrastructure to
the actual higher levels, which

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:12.170
are things like operational
and device security,

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:14.524
we ensure that we have all
those checkboxes checked,

00:11:14.524 --> 00:11:16.940
and we're making sure that you
embrace that security model

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:19.470
that we bring to the table.

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:22.310
